\begin_layout Standard
I had some bright idea yesterday on the train to Kaunas,
 but what was it?
 Oh right.
 Goes like this:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A distinguishing hallmark of biological organisms is that they can survive for billions of years.
 This is possible because the DNA is protected behind a cell wall,
 and because the DNA has error correction mechanisms of various kinds (double strand,
 but also windings around,
 and also explicit repair enzymes,
 and more...) Let backtrack this to single atoms.
 Better yet,
 a single oscillator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The context is again,
 here–and–now,
 and the question is:
 what is the developmental dynamics of the here and now?
 Its a dynamical system,
 of increasing complexity.
 Why?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Single atoms are unstable,
 in that they can be ionized.
 Ionization can be prevented by putting them behind a wall,
 a protection barrier.
 This barrier prevents causal inferences from the outside from harming the contents inside.
 The Feynman path integral says 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

integrate over all paths
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 The presence of a barrier says 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

no not all paths;
 there is no (direct) causal linkage between here and there.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (I mean,
 yes,
 of course this is not the intent of the path integral;
 I'm trying to evoke the origin of structure.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Rocks are subject to weathering;
 once dissolved or turned into sand,
 there's no going back.
 This is the conventional interpretation that 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

entropy always increases
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 or that the phase space of a bunch of sand is much much larger than that of the original rock.
 Or that rum and coke,
 once mixed,
 cannot be unmixed,
 purely as an entropic argument.
 In math,
 topological mixing or weak mixing or strong mixing.
 The presence of a biological cell wall prevents mixing across the wall.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The most basic dynamical system is an oscillator.
 The biological cell is a far more complex dynamical system.
 What drove it into being?
 It seems more stable,
 having autopoetic aspects.
 Maintenance of stability seems just as important as the growth of complexity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There's a tension:
 something is ingressing from the platonic world.
 For the case of evolution,
 the ingress is of structures that are thermodynamically reachable from the current cytoplasmic state.
 All possible states reachable from the present are explored.
 In the case of DNA,
 is this quantum or purely thermodynamic?
 Of course chemistry has a QM aspect to it,
 but does this matter for mutations?
 (Point mutations,
 cross–overs,
 and the far more complex processes involving jumping genes,
 methylation,
 Crispr,
 CAS,
 etc etc.) Here–and–now seems to be a purely quantum thing,
 right?
 To what degree is evolution volitional?
 Does evolution have a subjective experience?
 That is,
 does evolution intentionally select certain mutations,
 as opposed to being a purely random process driven by thermodynamic flows?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A collection of atoms will disperse,
 unless something binds them together.
 (viz,
 they will mix,
 rum–n–coke style) A collection of atoms with bonds,
 viz,
 a molecule,
 will be held together indefinitely,
 until such time that it is ionized.
 Molecules are form by the exchange of messages.
 In this case,
 the exchange of photons.
 Viz past light cone.
 And yet,
 these are all very infrared.
 That is,
 the system is diagonalizable.
 That is,
 the hydrogen atom,
 described by the Schrodinger eqn has a collection of atomic orbitals.
 The photons are implicit in the 
\begin_inset Formula $1/r^{2}$
\end_inset

 potential.
 That is,
 the soft photon exchange binding the electron to the proton has been replaced by a mean–field approximation;
 it nets out to 
\begin_inset Formula $1/r^{2}$
\end_inset

 by averaging over all possible photon exchanges.
 I do not know how to technically undo this;
 I don't know how (and have never seen) a derivation of the 
\begin_inset Formula $1/r^{2}$
\end_inset

 potential from first principles.
 I suppose that perhaps this is well–known,
 perhaps Feynman has demonstrated this,
 or someone else famous.
 I need to as google now.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Or not.
 I assume the derivation will be a mean–field one.
 Although it's confusing.
 Each emitted photon is also absorbed;
 each emitted photon carries momentum.
 The momentum can be canceled out by considering pairs of plane–wave photons traveling in opposite directions,
 or by using an s–wave,
 or spherical harmonics,
 which carry no momentum in the radial direction.
 But the absorbers are also quantum mechanical.
 How does one do mean–field,
 doesn't one need to ignore the absorbers?
 I'm imagining the ability to do an expansion in powers of hbar ...
 A core assumption of the derivation is that the photons are I.I.D.
 – independent identically distributed and this gives rise to normal QM.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The assumption of IID is foundational to QM.
 Can it be challenged?
 Certainly not in any kind of normal lab experiment.
 QM or tensor logic does have the idea of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

choice
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 but this choice is always random.
 There does not seem to be any room for willful,
 volitional choice at this level.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The only known mechanisms to complicate this picture re three–fold.
 The background space–time can be considered to be not smooth at the Planck scale.
 This can cause extremely tiny but 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

non–random
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 choices to be made.
 Then there is the cosmic neutrino background;
 the cross section is absurdly tiny but non–zero.
 Then there is the QFT vacuum.
 Even soft photons need to be dressed with multi–loop corrections.
 But in each of these,
 there is no obvious violation of I.I.D.
 and so no obvious source of volition or willfulness.
 Not onless one goes fully woo,
 and tries to assert willfullness in the universe,
 which somehow arranges the space–time background and the QED vacuum to do 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

just the right thing
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 for some reason.
 But this argument collapses ad absurdum;
 it just transfers willfulness to the universe,
 where it remains unexplainable and unanalyzable.
 This kind of woo may in fact exist,
 but it does not provide any kind of grip or crow–bar which can be applied to deconstruct,
 dismantle the core issue of volition.
 So this is a dead–end.
 At any rate,
 it also fails to tackle or address the idea of ingress.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
OK,
 next topic.
 Can we build a simply dynamical system that demonstrates ingress?
 As explained earlier,
 physical implementations of Turing machines are capable of ingressing:
 e.g.
 the Mersenne prime search will ingress a new prime when it is found.
 But Turing machines per se are not measure–preserving dynamical systems.
 Or are they?
 Well,
 it seems quite reasonable to describe a mechanical calculator;
 it would be measure–reserving.
 But then,
 the only reason that measures are interesting in the first place is to describe mixing and ergodicity;
 i.e.
 to take the 
\begin_inset Formula $N\to\infty$
\end_inset

 limit of some repeated operations.
 If this limit is not needed,
 then there is no reason to work with measures on a Cantor set.
 The measure could be supported on a finite set.
 Take a Turing machine with a finite tape,
 assign a finite measure to each cell.
 This gives a measure–preserving dynamical system that can ingress.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We conclude that ingress and measure are somehow unrelated?
 Lets say the machine is running code to find prime numbers.
 With each new prime that is discovered and printed to the tape,
 the complexity of the info on the tape increases.
 Technically,
 the entropy increases:
 if the tape started will all zeros,
 then this is an extremely ordered state,
 with very low entropy.
 With each new printed prime,
 one could argue that the entropy increased ....
 or did it?
 The primes are very not random.
 Err,
 hah hah.
 That is,
 with each new prime found,
 the contents of the tape changed,
 but not randomly.
 It remains in a very highly ordered state.
 There is no ensemble.
 Entropy is always computed for ensembles,
 and a deterministic machine does not offer an ensemble.
 Unless we use some Mealy–Moore equivalence to argue for a probabilistic machine,
 and thus an ensemble?
 The output,
 however,
 remains deterministic.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So we have a deterministic machine that is ingressing.
 The measure is preserved,
 the complexity increases.
 The entropy is ???
 Nothing quantum was needed.
 There was no volition,
 except for the ab initio question of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

where did this particular machine come from?
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

how did this machine manifest into the physical universe
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

?
 That is,
 there is some initial manifestation,
 outside of the scope of the stated question.
 This manifestation,
 once set to running,
 i.e.
 by allowing time to pass,
 increases the complexity of the physical universe,
 and causes prime numbers to ingress.
 That is,
 once the pump is primed,
 it can pump away all on it's own.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This raises two other issues:
 the only way to manifest a computer into the physical universe is to have a human being build it,
 and for that to happen,
 evolution must have run first.
 The only out to this might be the discovery of some astrophysical phenomenon that just so happens to look like an algo that computes primes.
 Maybe some weird earthquakes on some neutron star could do this.
 Very unlikely and implausible,
 but not impossible.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The other issue is representational or interpretational.
 That is,
 if a Turing machine is printing primes onto a tape,
 who 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

knows
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 that these are primes?
 Mathematicians and programmers might recognize them as such,
 but what if there were no humans around?
 In what sense are these numbers ingressing,
 if no one knows what they mean?
 The point here is that Levin is arguing that biological structures are ingressing,
 whereas I'm dealing with abstractions.
 But then,
 Levin is dealing with ingress from evolution;
 I'm dealing with ingress from mind.
\end_layout

