\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I have two choices in front of me.
 One is to ignore,
 bottle up the psychic pain that I feel,
 and move forward,
 stoically affectless,
 with the important tasks in front of me,
 e.g.
 buying clothing,
 examining finances,
 following up on some social obligations.
 The other is to do a dive into the disaffected vapour surrounding me,
 and do some emotional processing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I recognize the importance of the former,
 but I want to do the latter.
 The latter carries risks and rewards.
 The risk is that I fall into a state of moping,
 unhappy,
 dissatisfied,
 mildly depressed.
 The reward is that emotional processing,
 done without incurring self–injury,
 provides the foundation of meaning.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What is a meaningful life?
 Conventionally:
 to have lived,
 loved,
 learned.
 Ask your favorite LLM about this,
 and you'll get an answer along those lines:
 there must be millions of web pages and blogs,
 never mind books that discuss the nature of meaning and meaningful experience.
 I can provide no new insight;
 I process for myself.
 One of the curious things about a depressive state is that you can read advice for what you can do (what activities you can undertake) to lift your spirits,
 and all these activities seem so colorless and uninviting.
 There is not only a barrier between you and the happy state,
 but also a barrier that shuts out the advice.
 Oddly,
 I also want to write that the depressive state is deeply meaningful.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But first,
 a disclaimer:
 I'm not actually depressive,
 per se;
 I'm just nurturing,
 bathing such a state.
 There is a weak strain of feelings I am trying to grab a hold of and give voice to.
 The undercurrent is there;
 turning that undercurrent into words,
 into appropriate words is the challenge.
 I want to grab it while it is still there,
 while it is not gone.
 While it has not faded.
 While it is rich and colorful.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
And another aside before I dive to the main task.
 The background affect,
 for me,
 remains a weakening and fading angst of love.
 The lovesickness remains and colors and infuses the background.
 I am discovering the more I eat,
 the less my stomach hurts.
 I am also discovering that the more I eat,
 the more rapidly I gain weight.
 So,
 dicey.
 Exercise alone cannot burn those extra calories.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I'm not attempting to structure anything of what I am writing into any compelling literary narrative.
 This is more of a stream–of–consciousness flow.
 I have three or five topics I want to write of,
 right now,
 but of those,
 whenever I try to sort that list,
 to organize it,
 to weave it into a tapestry of development and narrative,
 I risk stalling out.
 It takes energy and effort to create a compelling narrative.
 I'm not writing a literary masterpiece here,
 for other (human) readers.
 I am trying to capture my feelings with words,
 and that has to be the primary effort:
 to splash the ineffable into text,
 in any way possible;
 a beautiful narrative with rational structure is necessarily secondary.
 Something else with bigger brains than I will be able to turn this into a Hollywood block–buster movie,
 if need me.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Meaning,
 the search for meaning,
 I want to project this onto everyone I meet,
 I have spent time with in Vilnius.
 I have the general impression that everyone is mildly unhappy,
 everyone is searching for meaning.
 My three day birthday party with trouble–on–wheels.
